  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "french-for-work run, 2026-11-13: written by hand for France, not yet checked by a second reader. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation (the 'German' tab). Words already in the lexicon are refs, with this job's own example and sense. Working conditions are general orientation only.",
  "intro": "Hotels and tourism employ many newcomers in France, and much of the work is seasonal. This deck covers reception and rooms, what housekeeping does every day, bookings and check-in, safety and cleaning, and what you say to guests and to your team. Say vous to guests; colleagues often say tu.",
  "introEasy": "Do you work in a hotel in France? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Réceptionniste",
    "Femme de chambre / valet de chambre",
    "Gouvernante",
    "Veilleur de nuit / veilleuse de nuit",
    "Bagagiste"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "At reception, a few sentences go a long way: « Bonjour madame, bienvenue. », « Vous avez une réservation ? », « Je peux voir votre pièce d'identité, s'il vous plaît ? », « Votre chambre est la 214. »",
    "Housekeeping uses its own words. A « recouche » is a room where the guest stays another night (light clean), « à blanc » is a full clean after departure. If you are not sure, ask the gouvernante: « C'est une recouche ou à blanc ? »",
    "Knock and say « Bonjour, ménage ! » before you open a door, and wait. If there is a « Ne pas déranger » sign, do not go in.",
    "Rules about contracts, seasonal work, guest registration and the taxe de séjour are set by law and by the convention collective. Ask your employer, France Travail or a union what applies to you. This is general orientation, not legal advice."
  ],
